  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="agents">
    <h2 id="agents">Agents</h2>
    <p>
      OpenOrc runs the agent CLIs you installed, signed in with your own accounts, and they connect to their services themselves. OpenOrc doesn't sit between an agent and its provider. It decides what a request starts with: your message, the
      conversation so far, and <a href={docHref("instructions")}>a short brief of its own</a>. The agent adds whatever it reads while it works, such as files and command output.
    </p>
    <p>
      The CLIs also make connections of their own, under their own terms: telemetry, their own update checks, and the MCP servers in your configuration. OpenOrc runs them for short jobs too, such as sign-in checks, model lists, usage,
      and skills; whether those go online is up to the CLI.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Claude Code connects the MCP servers from your Claude Code settings only in Autonomous, and only while <strong>Your Claude Code MCP servers</strong> is on under General. Other modes connect only OpenOrc's tools.</li>
      <li>Codex turns off the MCP servers in your Codex configuration only in Plan mode.</li>
      <li>In a copy of a pull request under review, no agent loads settings or MCP servers from the copied project.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The CLIs keep their own transcripts on your computer, such as <code>~/.claude/projects</code> for Claude Code and <code>~/.codex/sessions</code> for Codex. OpenOrc doesn't redact or delete them.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="background">
    <h2 id="background">Background requests</h2>
    <p>OpenOrc has no model of its own. When it needs one in the background, it runs an agent CLI once, in a new folder of its own inside your system's temporary folder, removed afterwards, and sends the prompt over standard input:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Thread titles</strong> send the first 1,500 characters of your first message and of the reply. By default the thread's own agent answers with a small model: Claude Haiku 4.5 for Claude Code, or a small Codex model, so the
        text stays with that provider. For an OpenCode thread, the small model can come from any provider you set up in OpenCode. A thread whose agent has no small model keeps its first line.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Code explanations</strong> send the file's path and its text, up to 160,000 characters. OpenOrc finds the other files that use it on your computer. The conversation's own agent answers with the model chosen under <strong>Text generation</strong>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Run summaries</strong> for memory send a digest of the run, up to 12,000 characters, with the thread's or task's title and a task's description. Memory is off in a new profile. Choosing a provider under Memory &amp; models
        sends every run's digest to that provider.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      For titles, explanations, and summaries, Claude Code runs with Bash, Edit, Write, WebFetch, and WebSearch turned off and without MCP servers, but can still read and search files. Codex runs in a read-only sandbox with no MCP servers, where it can
      still run commands that only read. OpenCode is denied every tool.
    </p>
    <p>
      For <strong>usage</strong>, Claude Code answers a usage request with its own sign-in, and Codex reads your account's limits through its app server. Pressing <strong>Use reset</strong> asks Codex to spend a reset. OpenOrc never reads
      the credentials these CLIs store, and doesn't read OpenCode's usage at all.
    </p>
    <p class="docs-source">Source: <code>packages/memory/src/text-generator.ts</code>, <code>packages/core/src/services/text-generation.ts</code>, <code>provider-usage.ts</code></p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="downloads">
    <h2 id="downloads">Downloads and update checks</h2>
    <h3>The embedding model</h3>
    <p>
      Memory's search by meaning uses a small model that runs on your computer, and it isn't part of the app. The first time memory needs it, OpenOrc downloads the model's files, 91 MB in all, from <code>huggingface.co</code>, which serves the largest through
      its download servers on <code>hf.co</code>. It keeps them only if each one matches a SHA-256 checksum written into OpenOrc's source. While memory is off, it is never downloaded.
    </p>
    <h3>App updates</h3>
    <p>
      Installed macOS and Windows releases ask GitHub Releases whether a newer version exists, including beta versions. Development builds and Linux releases never check. A check only compares versions: downloading and installing each wait
      until you choose them from the update notice or the app menu.
    </p>
    <p>The update library keeps a random ID in <code>.updaterId</code> in the data folder, meant for releasing to a share of installs first. OpenOrc doesn't release that way and sends the ID empty.</p>
    <h3>Agent updates</h3>
    <p>
      OpenOrc asks <code>registry.npmjs.org</code> for the latest version of each agent's package, <code>formulae.brew.sh</code> when a Homebrew cask installed Codex or Claude Code, or <code>downloads.claude.ai</code> for the release
      channel of a Claude Code that its own installer put in <code>~/.local/bin</code>. The request carries no cookies or credentials.
      Installing waits for you to press <strong>Update</strong>, and then runs the installer that put the agent there, such as <code>npm</code>, <code>brew</code>, or <code>claude update</code>.
    </p>
    <h3>Device tools</h3>
    <p>
      Turning on device support installs <code>expo-device-hub</code>, and <code>agent-device</code> when agents may use devices. OpenOrc runs your own <code>npm ci</code> with each tool's lockfile, so every package comes from your npm
      registry at a version and checksum fixed in OpenOrc's source, with install scripts turned off.
    </p>
    <p class="docs-source">Source: <code>packages/memory/src/model-files.ts</code>, <code>apps/desktop/src/main/app-updates.ts</code>, <code>packages/core/src/services/agent-update-installation.ts</code>, <code>packages/core/src/devices/toolchain.ts</code></p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="git">
    <h2 id="git">Git and GitHub</h2>
    <p>OpenOrc runs your own <code>git</code> and <code>gh</code>, so they use your credentials and configuration. Three things use the network without you pressing a Git button:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Creating a worktree from a branch fetches that branch from <code>origin</code> first, so the work starts from the remote branch. If the fetch fails, the local branch is used. Work in the Local checkout never fetches.</li>
      <li>A new worktree of a repository with submodules runs <code>git submodule update --init --recursive</code>, which can clone each submodule from its own remote.</li>
      <li>For each thread with an open pull request, OpenOrc runs <code>gh pr view &lt;url&gt; --json state</code> every 5 minutes to show when it is merged or closed.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Everything else happens when you ask: pushing, creating a pull request, listing and reading pull requests on the <a href={docHref("pull-requests")}>Pull requests</a> pages, fetching a pull request to review it, and posting a review.
      Before creating a pull request from a thread, OpenOrc checks that your branch is pushed using the remote branches Git already knows about, without going online.</p>
    <p class="docs-source">Source: <code>packages/core/src/services/workspace.ts</code>, <code>lifecycle.ts</code>, <code>review.ts</code>, <code>packages/git/src/github.ts</code>, <code>github-app.ts</code></p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="local">
    <h2 id="local">Local servers</h2>
    <p>These parts of OpenOrc listen for connections on <code>127.0.0.1</code>, so other computers can't reach them:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>OpenOrc's MCP server, which gives agents OpenOrc's tools, on a random port.</li>
      <li>
        The MCP Apps sandbox, which serves interactive tool results in isolated frames, on a random port. An app can load files from, and connect to, the <code>https</code> domains its MCP server declares, except <code>localhost</code>, <code>.local</code> names, and IP addresses.
      </li>
      <li>The Slack shared relay, on the computer that hosts it, at the port you set (47831 by default). Other users reach it through an SSH tunnel.</li>
      <li>The reviewer app's setup page, on a random port, only while you set the app up, for at most 15 minutes.</li>
      <li>OpenCode's own server, <code>opencode serve</code>, which OpenOrc starts briefly to list OpenCode's skills. It is protected by a password OpenCode makes up.</li>
      <li>On macOS, the update library's handoff to the system installer, on a random port behind a random password, while a downloaded update waits to install.</li>
      <li>While device support is on, <code>expo-device-hub</code> on a random port behind a token only OpenOrc holds, and <code>agent-device</code>'s helper, which OpenOrc and agents reach on <code>127.0.0.1</code> with its token.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      One listens on your network. While <strong>Allow paired phones</strong> is on, OpenOrc accepts phone connections on port 47820, or a free port when that one is taken. It drops any connection that doesn't come from this computer, a
      private or link-local address, or a Tailscale address before reading from it. Then only a paired phone can complete the encrypted handshake. See <a href={docHref("phone")}>Phone app</a>.
    </p>
    <p>The app window loads only files that ship with OpenOrc. Its content security policy blocks remote scripts, styles, fonts, and images.</p>
    <p>See <a href={docHref("security") + "#servers"}>Security model</a> for what keeps web pages and other programs out of these servers.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="cloud">
    <h2 id="cloud">Cloud threads and the brain</h2>
    <p>
      A cloud thread's sandbox and the cloud brain run on the Vercel or Agent37 account you connect in Settings → Cloud. Your computer talks to Vercel through its API, at <code>api.vercel.com</code> for your teams, projects, and costs, and at <code>vercel.com/api</code> for sandboxes. The sandbox calls
      also carry each command's input and output, including the connection to OpenOrc's runner inside the sandbox. On Agent37, it talks to OpenOrc's own server in each instance, at the instance's address, with your Agent37 key. Code moves both ways as Git bundles over these connections, never through a Git remote.
    </p>
    <p>From inside a sandbox, these connections go out:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The agent's own service, as on your computer. On Vercel, when your plan allows it, the sandbox's firewall adds the agent's sign-in to these requests on their way out.</li>
      <li><code>registry.npmjs.org</code>, through <code>npm</code>, to install the thread's agent at the version on your computer.</li>
      <li>Your system's package sources and <code>download.docker.com</code>, to install Docker for a project that uses it.</li>
      <li>Whatever the agent and its commands open. A sandbox's internet isn't limited.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The cloud brain makes the connections OpenOrc makes on a computer for the work it holds: the agents' services, its own sandboxes on the same account, GitHub if you switched its GitHub sign-in on, Apple's push service if it holds your push
      key, and Hugging Face for memory's model while memory is on. Paired phones reach the brain at its address. On Vercel they first knock on its door, a small function at <code>openorc-door-….vercel.app</code> in your team that wakes a sleeping brain.
    </p>
    <p>See <a href={docHref("cloud")}>Cloud threads</a> for what each service bills, and <a href={docHref("security") + "#cloud"}>Security model</a> for what the sandbox holds.</p>
    <p class="docs-source">Source: <code>packages/cloud/src/vercel-sandbox.ts</code>, <code>agent37.ts</code>, <code>instance-port.ts</code>, <code>agent-install.ts</code>, <code>docker.ts</code>, <code>brain/door.ts</code>, <code>packages/core/src/services/brain-link.ts</code></p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="never">
    <h2 id="never">What OpenOrc does not send</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>No usage analytics or crash reports. OpenOrc has no account of its own.</li>
      <li>No identifier for your installation with update checks or downloads.</li>
      <li>Nothing to an OpenOrc server, because there is none.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>OpenOrc does name itself: Codex and OpenCode see the client name <code>openorc</code>, and the reviewer app's requests to GitHub say <code>OpenOrc</code>. The <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> also covers this website, which does use analytics.</p>
    <Callout>What an agent connects to while it works, such as when it runs a command or fetches a page, depends on the agent and the thread's mode. See <a href={docHref("permissions")}>Modes and permissions</a>.</Callout>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="next-steps">
    <h2 id="next-steps">Next steps</h2>
    <LinkCards cards={[{ slug: "security" }, { slug: "storage" }, { slug: "how-it-works" }]} columns={3} />
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
